  "summary": "New spatial and single-cell tools assign signal to cell type, neighborhood or donor-level program, spanning segmentation-free inference to population-scale prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is attributing measured signal to specific biological sources: cell identity, spatial neighbors, or cell-type-specific programs. SSAF-T infers cell types without segmentation and uses them to guide segmentation, while SpaAxFormer models gene-resolved states across spatial neighbors for imputation. SPIN builds spatially informed cell-type co-expression networks for population-scale clinical prediction. Related cell-type-resolved work includes SEMPER, which aligns protein language model embeddings with interaction networks from 442 brain cell types, TriSCOPE, which links cell-type-specific Alzheimer's programs across ~4.2M nuclei from 627 donors, and scMINA, which prioritizes regulatory features in paired multiome data using XAI. The newer emphasis is reference-free or interpretable attribution that carries cell-type context into segmentation, imputation and clinical or regulatory inference, rather than treating segmentation as a fixed upstream step.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
